WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.558
[glass pops]

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.453
My name's Abdullah.

00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:14.594
I am a phone-repair-store owner.

00:00:14.594 --> 00:00:15.840
Today, I'll be showing you the repairs

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.323
of three different iPhones

00:00:17.323 --> 00:00:18.748
in three different conditions.

00:00:18.748 --> 00:00:21.440
They all have a similar
issue, a cracked back glass.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.130
The repairs are pretty similar,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.236
but each model has a few different steps.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.300
So, this iPhone XS definitely
has seen better days.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.380
Those chunks missing
on the top and bottom,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.721
those look a little scary.

00:00:38.721 --> 00:00:40.012
Water could get in there,

00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:42.320
debris could get in
there and damage things,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.920
but thankfully everything's
still working all right.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.090
The first thing we'll
do for this iPhone XS,

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.250
we'll put it in the laser machine

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.040
and we'll burn off the glue

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.840
that holds the glass onto the frame.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.810
IPhones come straight
from the Apple factory

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.140
with adhesive that holds the
back glass down to the frame,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.550
and it's my laser machine's
job to vaporize the glue

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.183
that's holding down the
glass to that frame.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.560
The laser burns at a
very high temperature,

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.618
enough to go through the glass,

00:01:10.618 --> 00:01:13.750
completely obliterate the
color standing in its way,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.886
and melts the glue straight off the frame.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.910
So, my laser machine
has a specific schematic

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.670
for each iPhone model.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.695
It's designed down to a microscopic level

00:01:26.695 --> 00:01:29.220
to avoid any gaps in the frame

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.033
and strictly go over the metal.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.020
Once all that adhesive is vaporized,

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.861
we'll take it out of the laser machine

00:01:36.861 --> 00:01:38.994
and we'll go over to our work bench

00:01:38.994 --> 00:01:41.276
and start cracking away this glass.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.180
When breaking off the back glass,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.671
we have to be super careful

00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:48.887
not to let the blade
puncture through the phone

00:01:48.887 --> 00:01:50.780
and damage an internal component.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.020
I use a series of tools
to remove the back glass.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.240
It depends on the damage.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.960
If an iPhone has big chunks
missing on the back glass

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.120
and it has easy access to
get underneath that glass,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.460
I'll just go ahead with a
blade similar to a scalpel,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.160
and I'll go in there and
I'll just pry off that glass.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.660
As you can see, the middle of the phone

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.770
still has color in the glass.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.695
Apple started using glass backs

00:02:21.695 --> 00:02:22.986
starting with the iPhone 8

00:02:22.986 --> 00:02:24.690
because of the wireless charging coil.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.650
That's the first model to have
the wireless charging coil.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.847
The reason why it needs a back glass

00:02:29.847 --> 00:02:31.450
is so that there isn't a piece of metal

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.030
that's affecting the
electricity going through

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.930
and charging the phone.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.590
The schematic in the laser machine

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.982
specifically omits the area

00:02:39.982 --> 00:02:42.007
over the wireless charging coil.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.795
During the second laser run, right here,

00:02:49.795 --> 00:02:52.068
you'll see we're vaporizing
the residual glue

00:02:52.068 --> 00:02:53.893
left over after removing the glass.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.438
Once the second laser round is done,

00:02:57.438 --> 00:02:59.446
we'll take it back over to our workstation

00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:02.258
and we'll brush off the rest of that glue.

00:03:02.258 --> 00:03:03.924
[brushing]

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.720
I use a metal brush that gets in

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.136
and scrubs that adhesive
right off of there.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.427
You have to have a surgeon's precision,

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:17.708
because if you go in and
get those metal brush teeth

00:03:17.708 --> 00:03:20.775
into a gap, you could damage a component.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.540
Now that I've removed all the glass,

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.440
I can finally start putting the phone

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.250
all the way back together.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.430
When I'm re-adhering the
back glass to the frame,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.496
I use industrial-strength glue.

00:03:33.496 --> 00:03:35.150
It's called B-7000.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.250
Straight from the Apple factory,

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.633
they use cold press glue.

00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:41.460
We try not to get too close to the edges.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.998
That way we don't have a lot of seepage.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.557
After the repair is done,

00:03:50.557 --> 00:03:52.040
if there's any extra glue,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.520
we'll go ahead and clean it
up with some glue remover.

00:03:57.435 --> 00:03:59.876
[glass crackling]

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.510
Moving on to the iPhone 8, as you can see,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.135
this one, it's cracked,

00:04:06.135 --> 00:04:07.993
but it's definitely not as bad as the XS.

00:04:07.993 --> 00:04:09.770
And it just has hairline
cracks all over the place,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.620
but thankfully it's
not missing any chunks,

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.320
so it looks like the inside
components should be fine.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.138
So, this back glass comes off

00:04:17.138 --> 00:04:19.990
almost exactly like how the iPhone XS did.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.330
The only difference is, with it being

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.670
the first iPhone that Apple
used the back glass on,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.290
they actually made the glue a
lot stronger for some reason.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.800
So, this repair, I tend
to go a little bit slower

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.608
when I'm breaking off the glass,

00:04:36.608 --> 00:04:38.883
and it tends to fling a lot farther

00:04:38.883 --> 00:04:41.032
just 'cause it's on there harder.

00:04:41.032 --> 00:04:43.507
[glass crackling]

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.010
One key difference with the
iPhone 8 back glass restoration

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.800
is that we have to be careful
around the flash module.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.083
As you can see there,

00:04:59.083 --> 00:05:02.325
there's a big hole to the
right of the camera lens,

00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:03.850
and unlike the iPhone XS,

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:07.220
where the flash is embedded
within the camera lens,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.565
this one is actually
placed off to the right,

00:05:10.565 --> 00:05:12.028
and it's exposed.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.020
Since the laser machine avoids
the wireless charging coil,

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.460
the adhesive that holds the glass above it

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.890
is actually still there,
so we have to slow down

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.661
and use a heat gun and
loosen up that adhesive.

00:05:24.661 --> 00:05:26.002
[heat gun whirring]

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.820
Right here we have the iPhone 12 Pro Max.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.170
This gold tends to hide
the cracks a little bit,

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.495
but you can see them.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.426
While fixing this 12 Pro Max,

00:05:58.426 --> 00:06:00.426
we'll follow the same basic steps

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:02.150
with any back glass repair.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.560
The key differences are the raised glass

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.543
in between the camera lenses

00:06:07.543 --> 00:06:10.268
and being careful when we're going around

00:06:10.268 --> 00:06:11.653
the MagSafe components.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.110
As you can see, the glass
is a little bit thicker

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.568
around the camera lenses.

00:06:20.568 --> 00:06:21.993
Starting with the iPhone 11,

00:06:21.993 --> 00:06:25.040
Apple started producing back glass

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.106
with glass that's about twice as thick

00:06:27.106 --> 00:06:28.800
around the camera lens.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.440
We do face an issue when we're
going to break that glass.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.880
Since it's thicker, it is
harder to get out of there.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.560
To get it out, we actually
have to fragment it.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.101
And to do that, we have punch tools,

00:06:40.101 --> 00:06:41.151
and this is similar to something

00:06:41.151 --> 00:06:44.650
that firefighters use
to break a windshield.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.420
Thankfully, this punch tool
is specifically designed

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.400
so that it only breaks
it on a surface level

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.140
and doesn't create enough pressure

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.143
to damage any internal components.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.000
With the iPhone 12 Pro
Max, we face a new issue,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.220
and that's the MagSafe.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.195
There are strips of magnets

00:07:01.195 --> 00:07:03.200
that go around the wireless charging coil,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.423
and those can be ripped off

00:07:05.423 --> 00:07:07.206
along with the glass when
you're pulling it off,

00:07:07.206 --> 00:07:08.750
so you have to be very careful

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:11.060
that we don't rip the wire connected

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.310
to those magnetic sections.

00:07:15.024 --> 00:07:17.507
[scrubbing]

00:07:32.225 --> 00:07:34.280
Although it might seem like
a nightmare of a process,

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:37.530
I find it really fun,
and it's also very fun

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.046
to see the before and afters.

00:07:39.046 --> 00:07:40.046
And that's one of the main reasons

00:07:40.046 --> 00:07:41.495
why I do what I do,

00:07:41.495 --> 00:07:43.070
just to see the transformation

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.178
of bringing things back to life.

